/-!
# E-merging functions

An e-merging function of finitely many e-values, indexed by a finite type `ι`, is a measurable
`F : (ι → ℝ≥0∞) → ℝ≥0∞` that turns e-variables into an e-variable, whatever their dependence.
It is defined here through laws: `∫⁻ e, F e ∂μ ≤ 1` for every probability measure `μ` on
`ι → ℝ≥0∞` whose coordinates have expectation at most `1`. This is equivalent to the definition
through random variables, `F ∘ E` being an e-variable for every null hypothesis on every measurable
space in the universe of `ι` and all e-variables `E i`
(`ProbabilityTheory.isEMerging_iff_forall_isEVar`), since the law of `E` under each member of the
null is such a `μ`; the forward direction holds on measurable spaces in any universe
(`ProbabilityTheory.IsEMerging.isEVar_comp`). No monotonicity is assumed, as in [Cle26].
Equivalently, an e-merging function is a bet on the space of values: an e-variable for the null
`mergingNull univ` of the joint laws of e-values
(`ProbabilityTheory.isEMerging_iff_isEVar_mergingNull`), and so an e-variable for `mergingNull 𝒞`
for every set `𝒞` of joint laws. Every e-variable for `mergingNull 𝒞` merges e-variables whose
joint laws lie in `𝒞` (`ProbabilityTheory.IsEVar.comp_isEVar`): the merging functions for
independent and for exchangeable e-values, in `EValues/Merging/Independent` and
`EValues/Merging/Exchangeable`, are the e-variables of the merging nulls of the product laws and of
the laws invariant under the permutations of the coordinates.

The weighted means `e ↦ w₀ + ∑ i, w i * e i`, with nonnegative weights summing to `1`, are
e-merging, and every e-merging function is dominated by one of them on finite arguments
[Wan25, Theorem 1; Cle26; RW, Theorem 8.4]. Values at infinite arguments are irrelevant, since
e-variables are almost surely finite (`ProbabilityTheory.IsEMerging.of_le`), so domination and
admissibility are statements about finite arguments `e : ι → ℝ≥0`. The admissible e-merging
functions are therefore exactly the weighted means. For symmetric e-merging functions, the weights
of the coordinates can be taken equal, which gives the domination by `λ + (1 - λ) M` with `M` the
arithmetic mean, the characterization of the admissible ones, and the essential domination of
every symmetric e-merging function by `M` [VW21, Proposition 3.1, Theorem 3.2; RW,
Proposition 8.3].

## Main definitions

* `ProbabilityTheory.IsEMerging F`: `F` is an e-merging function.
* `ProbabilityTheory.mergingNull 𝒞`: the probability measures of a set `𝒞` of measures on
  `ι → ℝ≥0∞` whose coordinates have expectation at most `1`.
* `ProbabilityTheory.IsAdmissibleEMerging F`: `F` is an admissible e-variable for
  `mergingNull univ`.

## Main statements

* `ProbabilityTheory.isEMerging_iff_isEVar_mergingNull`: the e-merging functions are the
  e-variables of `mergingNull univ`, and `ProbabilityTheory.IsEMerging.isEVar_mergingNull`: they
  are e-variables of `mergingNull 𝒞` for every `𝒞`.
* `ProbabilityTheory.mapNull_subset_mergingNull_iff`: the joint laws of random variables lie in
  `mergingNull 𝒞` exactly when the variables are e-variables with joint laws in `𝒞`, and
  `ProbabilityTheory.IsEVar.comp_isEVar`: an e-variable for `mergingNull 𝒞` merges e-variables
  whose joint laws lie in `𝒞`. The validity of e-merging functions, and of the merging functions
  for independent and for exchangeable e-values, are its instances.
* `ProbabilityTheory.quasiSure_mergingNull_univ_eq_principal`: the quasi-sure filter of
  `mergingNull univ` is the principal filter of the points with finite coordinates.
* `ProbabilityTheory.IsEVar.mono_mergingNull`: a measurable function dominated on finite arguments
  by an e-variable for a merging null is one.
* `ProbabilityTheory.mergingNull_univ_subset_constraintNull`,
  `ProbabilityTheory.mergingNull_univ_ssubset_constraintNull`: the null `mergingNull univ` is
  contained in, and strictly smaller than, the null generated by the real constraints
  `e ↦ (e i).toReal - 1`.
* `ProbabilityTheory.IsEMerging.isEVar_comp`: an e-merging function of e-variables is an
  e-variable, for every null of probability measures;
  `ProbabilityTheory.isEMerging_iff_forall_isEVar` characterizes e-merging functions by this
  property.
* `ProbabilityTheory.isEMerging_add_sum_mul`: the weighted means are e-merging.
* `ProbabilityTheory.IsEMerging.exists_le_add_sum_mul`: every e-merging function is dominated by a
  weighted mean on finite arguments [Wan25, Theorem 1; Cle26], from the complete class theorem
  [Cle26b; LRR26; EVR26], and
  `ProbabilityTheory.isEMerging_iff_exists_le_add_sum_mul`: this characterizes e-merging functions
  among measurable functions.
* `ProbabilityTheory.isAdmissibleEMerging_iff_isAdmissible`: an admissible e-merging function is
  one that every e-merging function dominating it at finite arguments equals there
  (`ProbabilityTheory.IsAdmissibleEMerging.eq_of_le`).
* `ProbabilityTheory.isAdmissibleEMerging_iff`: the admissible e-merging functions are the weighted
  means on finite arguments [Wan25, Theorem 1; RW, Theorem 8.4], and
  `ProbabilityTheory.IsEMerging.exists_isAdmissibleEMerging_le`: every e-merging function is
  dominated by an admissible one.
* `ProbabilityTheory.isMinimalCompleteClass_add_sum_mul`: the weighted means form a minimal
  complete class of e-merging functions.
* `ProbabilityTheory.IsEMerging.exists_le_add_mul_sum_div_card`: a symmetric e-merging function is
  dominated by `λ + (1 - λ) M` for some `λ ∈ [0, 1]` [VW21, Theorem 3.2], and
  `ProbabilityTheory.isAdmissibleEMerging_iff_of_symmetric`: the symmetric admissible ones are
  exactly these functions.
* `ProbabilityTheory.IsEMerging.le_sum_div_card_of_symmetric`: the arithmetic mean essentially
  dominates every symmetric e-merging function: `F e ≤ M e` wherever `1 < F e` [VW21,
  Proposition 3.1; RW, Proposition 8.3].

## Implementation notes

The weights dominating an e-merging function `F` come from the complete class theorem in
finite-dimensional form (`ProbabilityTheory.exists_mem_nonredundantWeights_le_of_noArbitrageOn`)
on the space `ι → ℝ≥0` of finite values, for the constraints `e ↦ e i - 1`, which are all negative
at `0` and so admit no arbitrage, and the function `e ↦ F e - 1` [Cle26b, closing remark], as in
[EVR26]. Its
hypothesis is the e-merging property under the finitely supported laws on the finite values. The
derivation uses the finite values: on `ι → ℝ≥0∞`, the null generated by the real constraints
`e ↦ (e i).toReal - 1` contains the Dirac mass at the point with infinite coordinates, which is not
in `mergingNull univ` (`ProbabilityTheory.mergingNull_univ_ssubset_constraintNull`, following
[EVR26]).

## References

* [RW] A. Ramdas and R. Wang, *Hypothesis Testing with E-values*, Foundations and Trends in
  Statistics 1 (2025), arXiv:2410.23614v6: Definition 8.1, Propositions 8.3 and A.3,
  Theorem 8.4.
* [VW21] V. Vovk and R. Wang, *E-values: calibration, combination and applications*, Ann. Statist.
  49 (2021), arXiv:1912.06116v4: Proposition 3.1, Theorem 3.2, Proposition E.5.
* [Wan25] R. Wang, *The only admissible way of merging arbitrary e-values*, Biometrika (2025),
  arXiv:2409.19888v2: Theorem 1.
* [Cle26] E. Clerico, *A simple geometric proof for the characterisation of e-merging functions*,
  arXiv:2512.09708v3.
* [Cle26b] E. Clerico, *Sequential testing of conditionally constrained hypotheses*,
  arXiv:2606.06769v2: closing remark.
* [EVR26] *Answers to open questions in e-values and anytime-valid inference*, the Lean library
  `EValuesResearch` of github.com/RemyDegenne/evalues_research (2026): the derivation of the
  domination by weighted means from the complete class theorem, and the Dirac mass at the point
  with infinite coordinates showing that the real constraints `e ↦ (e i).toReal - 1` generate a
  larger null than `mergingNull univ`, both in `notes/book/Checks.lean`, are adapted here.
* [LRR26] M. Larsson, A. Ramdas and J. Ruf, *Testing hypotheses generated by constraints*, Math.
  Oper. Res. (2026), arXiv:2504.02974v5: Theorem 3.1.
-/

public section

open MeasureTheory
open scoped ENNReal NNReal

namespace ProbabilityTheory

universe u

variable {ι : Type*} [Fintype ι] {F G : (ι → ℝ≥0∞) → ℝ≥0∞}

/-- An **e-merging function** of e-values indexed by `ι` [RW, Definition 8.1, Proposition A.3;
Cle26]: a measurable `F : (ι → ℝ≥0∞) → ℝ≥0∞` with `∫⁻ e, F e ∂μ ≤ 1` for every probability
measure `μ` on `ι → ℝ≥0∞` whose coordinates have expectation at most `1`. It is not required to be
monotone. -/
structure IsEMerging (F : (ι → ℝ≥0∞) → ℝ≥0∞) : Prop where
  measurable : Measurable F
  lintegral_le_one : ∀ (μ : Measure (ι → ℝ≥0∞)) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ],
    (∀ i, ∫⁻ e, e i ∂μ ≤ 1) → ∫⁻ e, F e ∂μ ≤ 1

/-! ### The null of the joint laws of e-values -/

section MergingNull

variable {𝒞 𝒟 : Set (Measure (ι → ℝ≥0∞))} {μ : Measure (ι → ℝ≥0∞)}

omit [Fintype ι]

/-- The **merging null** of a set `𝒞` of measures on `ι → ℝ≥0∞`: the probability measures of `𝒞`
whose coordinates have expectation at most `1`, the joint laws in `𝒞` of `ι` e-values. The
e-merging functions are the e-variables of `mergingNull univ`
(`ProbabilityTheory.isEMerging_iff_isEVar_mergingNull`); a smaller set `𝒞` of joint laws, such as
the products of laws or the laws invariant under the permutations of the coordinates, gives the
merging functions for e-values with that dependence structure. -/
def mergingNull (𝒞 : Set (Measure (ι → ℝ≥0∞))) : Set (Measure (ι → ℝ≥0∞)) :=
  𝒞 ∩ {μ | IsProbabilityMeasure μ ∧ ∀ i, ∫⁻ e, e i ∂μ ≤ 1}

@[simp]
theorem mem_mergingNull :
    μ ∈ mergingNull 𝒞 ↔ μ ∈ 𝒞 ∧ IsProbabilityMeasure μ ∧ ∀ i, ∫⁻ e, e i ∂μ ≤ 1 :=
  Iff.rfl

/-- The members of a merging null are probability measures. -/
theorem isProbabilityMeasure_of_mem_mergingNull (hμ : μ ∈ mergingNull 𝒞) :
    IsProbabilityMeasure μ :=
  (mem_mergingNull.1 hμ).2.1

/-- The merging null of `𝒞` consists of members of `𝒞`. -/
theorem mergingNull_subset (𝒞 : Set (Measure (ι → ℝ≥0∞))) : mergingNull 𝒞 ⊆ 𝒞 :=
  Set.inter_subset_left

/-- The merging null is monotone in the set of joint laws. -/
theorem mergingNull_mono (h : 𝒞 ⊆ 𝒟) : mergingNull 𝒞 ⊆ mergingNull 𝒟 :=
  Set.inter_subset_inter_left _ h

/-- The coordinates are e-variables for every merging null. -/
theorem isEVar_eval_mergingNull (𝒞 : Set (Measure (ι → ℝ≥0∞))) (i : ι) :
    IsEVar (fun e : ι → ℝ≥0∞ ↦ e i) (mergingNull 𝒞) :=
  ⟨measurable_pi_apply i, fun _ hμ ↦ (mem_mergingNull.1 hμ).2.2 i⟩

/-- The finitely supported members of the merging null of all measures: the measure with weights
`w k` at the points `x k` is a member exactly when the weights sum to `1` and every coordinate has a
mean at most `1`. -/
theorem sum_smul_dirac_mem_mergingNull_univ_iff {α : Type*} [Fintype α]
    {w : α → ℝ≥0∞} {x : α → ι → ℝ≥0∞} :
    ∑ k, w k • Measure.dirac (x k) ∈ mergingNull Set.univ ↔
      ∑ k, w k = 1 ∧ ∀ i, ∑ k, w k * x k i ≤ 1 := by
  have hint (i : ι) : ∫⁻ e, e i ∂(∑ k, w k • Measure.dirac (x k)) = ∑ k, w k * x k i := by
    rw [lintegral_finsetSum_measure]
    simp [lintegral_dirac' _ (measurable_pi_apply i : Measurable fun e : ι → ℝ≥0∞ ↦ e i)]
  simp [isProbabilityMeasure_iff, hint]

/-- The merging null of all measures lies in the null generated by the real constraints
`e ↦ (e i).toReal - 1`. -/
theorem mergingNull_univ_subset_constraintNull :
    mergingNull Set.univ ⊆
      constraintNull (Set.range fun (i : ι) (e : ι → ℝ≥0∞) ↦ (e i).toReal - 1) := by
  intro μ hμ
  obtain ⟨-, hμ1, hμe⟩ := mem_mergingNull.1 hμ
  refine mem_constraintNull_range_iff.2 ⟨hμ1, fun i ↦ ?_⟩
  have hm : Measurable fun e : ι → ℝ≥0∞ ↦ e i := measurable_pi_apply i
  have hfin : ∫⁻ e, e i ∂μ ≠ ∞ := ((hμe i).trans_lt ENNReal.one_lt_top).ne
  have hint := integrable_toReal_of_lintegral_ne_top hm.aemeasurable hfin
  refine ⟨hint.sub (integrable_const 1), ?_⟩
  rw [integral_sub hint (integrable_const 1), integral_toReal hm.aemeasurable (ae_lt_top hm hfin)]
  simpa using ENNReal.toReal_le_of_le_ofReal zero_le_one (by simpa using hμe i)

/-- The merging null of all measures is strictly smaller than the null generated by the real
constraints `e ↦ (e i).toReal - 1` when there is at least one e-value: these constraints do not see
the value `∞`, so the Dirac mass at the point with infinite coordinates satisfies them, while its
coordinates do not have expectation at most `1`. The argument is adapted from [EVR26]. -/
theorem mergingNull_univ_ssubset_constraintNull [Nonempty ι] :
    mergingNull Set.univ ⊂
      constraintNull (Set.range fun (i : ι) (e : ι → ℝ≥0∞) ↦ (e i).toReal - 1) := by
  refine ⟨mergingNull_univ_subset_constraintNull, fun h ↦ ?_⟩
  obtain ⟨i⟩ := ‹Nonempty ι›
  have hmem : Measure.dirac (fun _ : ι ↦ (∞ : ℝ≥0∞)) ∈ constraintNull
      (Set.range fun (i : ι) (e : ι → ℝ≥0∞) ↦ (e i).toReal - 1) := by
    rw [dirac_mem_constraintNull_iff (by rintro _ ⟨i, rfl⟩; fun_prop)]
    rintro _ ⟨i, rfl⟩
    simp
  have hi := (mem_mergingNull.1 (h hmem)).2.2 i
  rw [lintegral_dirac' _ (measurable_pi_apply i)] at hi
  exact ENNReal.top_ne_one (le_antisymm hi le_top)

/-- Every point with finite coordinates is charged by a member of the merging null of all
measures: the mixture of the Dirac masses at `0` and at `e`, with weight `(1 + ∑ i, e i)⁻¹` at
`e`. -/
theorem exists_mem_mergingNull_univ_measure_singleton_ne_zero [Finite ι] (e : ι → ℝ≥0) :
    ∃ μ ∈ mergingNull Set.univ, μ {fun i ↦ (e i : ℝ≥0∞)} ≠ 0 := by
  have := Fintype.ofFinite ι
  set s : ℝ≥0∞ := 1 + ∑ i, (e i : ℝ≥0∞)
  have hs : s ≠ ∞ := by simp [s]
  have hs0 : s ≠ 0 := by simp [s]
  set p := s⁻¹
  have hp1 : p ≤ 1 := ENNReal.inv_le_one.2 le_self_add
  refine ⟨(1 - p) • Measure.dirac 0 + p • Measure.dirac (fun i ↦ (e i : ℝ≥0∞)),
    mem_mergingNull.2 ⟨Set.mem_univ _, ⟨by simp [tsub_add_cancel_of_le hp1]⟩, fun i ↦ ?_⟩, ?_⟩
  · simp only [lintegral_add_measure, lintegral_smul_measure, lintegral_dirac, smul_eq_mul]
    calc (1 - p) * (0 : ι → ℝ≥0∞) i + p * e i ≤ p * s := by
          rw [Pi.zero_apply, mul_zero, zero_add]
          gcongr
          exact le_add_left (Finset.single_le_sum (f := fun i ↦ (e i : ℝ≥0∞))
            (fun _ _ ↦ zero_le) (Finset.mem_univ i))
      _ = 1 := ENNReal.inv_mul_cancel hs0 hs
  · simp [p, hs]

/-- **The quasi-sure filter of the merging null of all measures** is the principal filter of the
points with finite coordinates: the coordinates are almost surely finite under every member, and
every point with finite coordinates is charged by some member
(`exists_mem_mergingNull_univ_measure_singleton_ne_zero`). -/
theorem quasiSure_mergingNull_univ_eq_principal [Finite ι] :
    quasiSure (mergingNull (Set.univ : Set (Measure (ι → ℝ≥0∞)))) =
      Filter.principal (Set.range fun (e : ι → ℝ≥0) i ↦ (e i : ℝ≥0∞)) := by
  refine Filter.ext fun s ↦ ⟨fun h ↦ ?_, fun h ↦ mem_quasiSure_iff.2 fun μ hμ ↦ ?_⟩
  · rintro _ ⟨e, rfl⟩
    by_contra hes
    obtain ⟨μ, hμ, hμe⟩ := exists_mem_mergingNull_univ_measure_singleton_ne_zero e
    exact hμe (measure_mono_null (Set.singleton_subset_iff.2 hes)
      (mem_ae_iff.1 (mem_quasiSure_iff.1 h μ hμ)))
  · filter_upwards [ae_all_iff.2 fun i ↦ (isEVar_eval_mergingNull Set.univ i).ae_ne_top hμ]
      with x hx
    exact h ⟨fun i ↦ (x i).toNNReal, funext fun i ↦ ENNReal.coe_toNNReal (hx i)⟩

/-- **E-merging functions are the e-variables of the merging null**: a merging function is a bet
on the space of values, against the null of the joint laws of e-values. -/
theorem isEMerging_iff_isEVar_mergingNull : IsEMerging F ↔ IsEVar F (mergingNull Set.univ) :=
  ⟨fun hF ↦ ⟨hF.measurable, fun _ hμ ↦ have := isProbabilityMeasure_of_mem_mergingNull hμ;
      hF.lintegral_le_one _ (mem_mergingNull.1 hμ).2.2⟩,
    fun hF ↦ ⟨hF.measurable, fun μ _ hμ ↦
      hF.lintegral_le_one (mem_mergingNull.2 ⟨Set.mem_univ μ, ‹_›, hμ⟩)⟩⟩

/-- An e-merging function is a merging function for every set of joint laws of e-values: it is an
e-variable for every merging null. -/
theorem IsEMerging.isEVar_mergingNull (hF : IsEMerging F) (𝒞 : Set (Measure (ι → ℝ≥0∞))) :
    IsEVar F (mergingNull 𝒞) :=
  (isEMerging_iff_isEVar_mergingNull.1 hF).anti_set (mergingNull_mono (Set.subset_univ 𝒞))

variable {Ω : Type*} {mΩ : MeasurableSpace Ω} {S : Set (Measure Ω)} {E : ι → Ω → ℝ≥0∞}

/-- **The joint laws of e-variables.** For a null of probability measures and measurable `E i`,
the joint laws of the `E i` under the members of the null lie in the merging null of `𝒞` exactly
when the `E i` are e-variables whose joint laws lie in `𝒞`. -/
theorem mapNull_subset_mergingNull_iff (hS : ∀ P ∈ S, IsProbabilityMeasure P)
    (hE : ∀ i, Measurable (E i)) :
    mapNull (fun ω i ↦ E i ω) S ⊆ mergingNull 𝒞 ↔
      (∀ i, IsEVar (E i) S) ∧ ∀ P ∈ S, P.map (fun ω i ↦ E i ω) ∈ 𝒞 := by
  have hm : Measurable fun ω i ↦ E i ω := measurable_pi_iff.2 hE
  refine ⟨fun h ↦ ⟨fun i ↦ ?_, fun P hP ↦ mergingNull_subset 𝒞 (h (map_mem_mapNull hP))⟩, ?_⟩
  · -- `E i` is the coordinate `i`, an e-variable for the merging null, read through the joint law
    exact (isEVar_mapNull_iff hm (measurable_pi_apply i)).1
      ((isEVar_eval_mergingNull 𝒞 i).anti_set h)
  · rintro ⟨hEv, h𝒞⟩ _ hμ
    obtain ⟨P, hP, rfl⟩ := mem_mapNull.1 hμ
    have := hS P hP
    refine mem_mergingNull.2 ⟨h𝒞 P hP, inferInstance, fun i ↦ ?_⟩
    rw [lintegral_map (measurable_pi_apply i) hm]
    exact (hEv i).lintegral_le_one hP

/-- **Merging e-variables.** An e-variable `F` for the merging null of a set `𝒞` of joint laws,
applied to e-variables whose joint law under every member of a null of probability measures lies
in `𝒞`, is an e-variable. With `𝒞` the set of all measures, the products of laws, or the laws
invariant under the permutations of the coordinates, this is the validity of the merging functions
for arbitrarily dependent, independent, or exchangeable e-values. -/
theorem IsEVar.comp_isEVar (hF : IsEVar F (mergingNull 𝒞)) (hS : ∀ P ∈ S, IsProbabilityMeasure P)
    (hE : ∀ i, IsEVar (E i) S) (h𝒞 : ∀ P ∈ S, P.map (fun ω i ↦ E i ω) ∈ 𝒞) :
    IsEVar (fun ω ↦ F fun i ↦ E i ω) S :=
  (isEVar_mapNull_iff (measurable_pi_iff.2 fun i ↦ (hE i).measurable) hF.measurable).1
    (hF.anti_set ((mapNull_subset_mergingNull_iff hS fun i ↦ (hE i).measurable).2 ⟨hE, h𝒞⟩))

end MergingNull

omit [Fintype ι] in
/-- An e-merging function of e-variables is an e-variable, for every null hypothesis of
probability measures, with no assumption on the dependence of the e-variables. -/
theorem IsEMerging.isEVar_comp {Ω : Type*} {mΩ : MeasurableSpace Ω} {S : Set (Measure Ω)}
    (hF : IsEMerging F) (hS : ∀ P ∈ S, IsProbabilityMeasure P) {E : ι → Ω → ℝ≥0∞}
    (hE : ∀ i, IsEVar (E i) S) : IsEVar (fun ω ↦ F fun i ↦ E i ω) S :=
  (isEMerging_iff_isEVar_mergingNull.1 hF).comp_isEVar hS hE fun _ _ ↦ Set.mem_univ _

/-- A function of e-values is e-merging exactly when it turns e-variables into an e-variable, for
every null hypothesis of probability measures on every measurable space in the universe of `ι`.
The forward direction holds on measurable spaces in any universe
(`ProbabilityTheory.IsEMerging.isEVar_comp`). -/
theorem isEMerging_iff_forall_isEVar {ι : Type u} {F : (ι → ℝ≥0∞) → ℝ≥0∞} :
    IsEMerging F ↔ ∀ (Ω : Type u) [MeasurableSpace Ω] (S : Set (Measure Ω)),
      (∀ P ∈ S, IsProbabilityMeasure P) → ∀ E : ι → Ω → ℝ≥0∞, (∀ i, IsEVar (E i) S) →
        IsEVar (fun ω ↦ F fun i ↦ E i ω) S := by
  refine ⟨fun hF Ω _ S hS E hE ↦ hF.isEVar_comp hS hE, fun h ↦ ?_⟩
  -- Apply the hypothesis to the coordinates, under the merging null.
  exact isEMerging_iff_isEVar_mergingNull.2 <| h (ι → ℝ≥0∞) (mergingNull Set.univ)
    (fun _ ↦ isProbabilityMeasure_of_mem_mergingNull) (fun i e ↦ e i)
    (isEVar_eval_mergingNull Set.univ)

omit [Fintype ι] in
/-- An e-merging function of finitely many e-values is finite at finite arguments. -/
theorem IsEMerging.lt_top [Finite ι] (hF : IsEMerging F) (e : ι → ℝ≥0) :
    F (fun i ↦ e i) < ∞ := by
  -- Under a member `μ` of the merging null charging `e`, `F e * μ {e} ≤ ∫⁻ F ∂μ ≤ 1`.
  obtain ⟨μ, hμ, hμe⟩ := exists_mem_mergingNull_univ_measure_singleton_ne_zero e
  have hle : F (fun i ↦ e i) * μ {fun i ↦ (e i : ℝ≥0∞)} ≤ 1 :=
    ((lintegral_singleton' hF.measurable _).symm.trans_le (setLIntegral_le_lintegral _ _)).trans
      ((isEMerging_iff_isEVar_mergingNull.1 hF).lintegral_le_one hμ)
  refine lt_top_iff_ne_top.2 fun htop ↦ ?_
  rw [htop, ENNReal.top_mul hμe] at hle
  exact ENNReal.top_ne_one (le_antisymm hle le_top)

omit [Fintype ι] in
/-- A function dominated on finite arguments by `G` is dominated by `G` almost everywhere, under
every measure on `ι → ℝ≥0∞` whose coordinates are almost everywhere finite. -/
theorem ae_le_of_forall_nnreal_le {ν : Measure (ι → ℝ≥0∞)} (hfin : ∀ᵐ e ∂ν, ∀ i, e i ≠ ∞)
    (hle : ∀ e : ι → ℝ≥0, F (fun i ↦ e i) ≤ G (fun i ↦ e i)) : F ≤ᵐ[ν] G := by
  filter_upwards [hfin] with e he
  have : e = fun i ↦ ((e i).toNNReal : ℝ≥0∞) := funext fun i ↦ (ENNReal.coe_toNNReal (he i)).symm
  rw [this]
  exact hle _

omit [Fintype ι] in
/-- A measurable function dominated on finite arguments by an e-variable for a merging null is an
e-variable for it: the values at infinite arguments are irrelevant, since the coordinates are
almost surely finite under every member of the merging null. -/
theorem IsEVar.mono_mergingNull [Countable ι] {𝒞 : Set (Measure (ι → ℝ≥0∞))}
    (hG : IsEVar G (mergingNull 𝒞)) (hF : Measurable F)
    (hle : ∀ e : ι → ℝ≥0, F (fun i ↦ e i) ≤ G (fun i ↦ e i)) : IsEVar F (mergingNull 𝒞) :=
  hG.mono_ae hF (eventuallyLE_quasiSure_iff.2 fun _ hμ ↦ ae_le_of_forall_nnreal_le
    (ae_all_iff.2 fun i ↦ (isEVar_eval_mergingNull 𝒞 i).ae_ne_top hμ) hle)

omit [Fintype ι] in
/-- A measurable function dominated on finite arguments by an e-merging function is e-merging:
the values of an e-merging function at infinite arguments are irrelevant, since the
coordinates of `μ` are `μ`-almost surely finite. -/
theorem IsEMerging.of_le [Countable ι] (hG : IsEMerging G) (hF : Measurable F)
    (hle : ∀ e : ι → ℝ≥0, F (fun i ↦ e i) ≤ G (fun i ↦ e i)) : IsEMerging F :=
  isEMerging_iff_isEVar_mergingNull.2
    ((isEMerging_iff_isEVar_mergingNull.1 hG).mono_mergingNull hF hle)

/-- The **weighted means** `e ↦ w₀ + ∑ i, w i * e i`, with nonnegative weights of total mass at
most `1`, are e-merging. -/
theorem isEMerging_add_sum_mul {w₀ : ℝ≥0} {w : ι → ℝ≥0} (hw : w₀ + ∑ i, w i ≤ 1) :
    IsEMerging fun e : ι → ℝ≥0∞ ↦ (w₀ : ℝ≥0∞) + ∑ i, (w i : ℝ≥0∞) * e i := by
  refine ⟨by fun_prop, fun μ _ he ↦ ?_⟩
  rw [lintegral_add_left measurable_const, lintegral_const, measure_univ, mul_one,
    lintegral_finsetSum _ fun i _ ↦ (measurable_pi_apply i).const_mul _]
  rw [Finset.sum_congr rfl fun i _ ↦
    lintegral_const_mul _ (measurable_pi_apply i : Measurable fun e : ι → ℝ≥0∞ ↦ e i)]
  calc (w₀ : ℝ≥0∞) + ∑ i, (w i : ℝ≥0∞) * ∫⁻ e, e i ∂μ ≤ w₀ + ∑ i, (w i : ℝ≥0∞) * 1 := by
        gcongr with i
        exact he i
    _ ≤ 1 := by simp only [mul_one]; exact_mod_cast hw

/-- The weighted mean of finite arguments has the same value computed in `ℝ≥0` and in `ℝ≥0∞`. -/
private lemma coe_add_sum_mul (w₀ : ℝ≥0) (w e : ι → ℝ≥0) :
    ((w₀ + ∑ i, w i * e i : ℝ≥0) : ℝ≥0∞) = w₀ + ∑ i, (w i : ℝ≥0∞) * e i := by
  rw [ENNReal.coe_add, ENNReal.ofNNReal_finsetSum]
  simp only [ENNReal.coe_mul]

omit [Fintype ι] in
/-- Under a finitely supported law on the finite values whose coordinates have means at most `1`,
an e-merging function has a mean at most `1`: the hypothesis of the complete class theorem for the
constraints `e ↦ e i - 1` and the function `e ↦ F e - 1`. -/
private lemma IsEMerging.sum_mul_toReal_sub_one_nonpos [Finite ι] (hF : IsEMerging F) {α : Type*}
    [Fintype α] (x : α → ι → ℝ≥0) {w : α → ℝ} (hw : w ∈ LMLExplore.simplex α)
    (hwx : ∀ i, ∑ k, w k * ((x k i : ℝ) - 1) ≤ 0) :
    ∑ k, w k * ((F fun i ↦ (x k i : ℝ≥0∞)).toReal - 1) ≤ 0 := by
  obtain ⟨hw0, hw1⟩ := LMLExplore.mem_simplex_iff.1 hw
  -- The law with the weights `w k` at the points `x k` is in the merging null.
  have hsum : ∑ k, ENNReal.ofReal (w k) = 1 := by
    rw [← ENNReal.ofReal_sum_of_nonneg fun k _ ↦ hw0 k, hw1, ENNReal.ofReal_one]
  have hcoord (i : ι) : ∑ k, ENNReal.ofReal (w k) * (x k i : ℝ≥0∞) ≤ 1 :=
    LMLExplore.sum_ofReal_mul_le_one_of_sum_mul_toReal_sub_one_nonpos hw
      (fun _ ↦ ENNReal.coe_ne_top) (by simpa using hwx i)
  have hle := (hF.isEVar_mergingNull Set.univ).lintegral_le_one
    ((sum_smul_dirac_mem_mergingNull_univ_iff (w := fun k ↦ ENNReal.ofReal (w k))
      (x := fun k i ↦ (x k i : ℝ≥0∞))).2 ⟨hsum, hcoord⟩)
  rw [LMLExplore.lintegral_sum_smul_dirac] at hle
  exact LMLExplore.sum_mul_toReal_sub_one_nonpos_of_sum_ofReal_mul_le_one hw hle

/-- **Every e-merging function is dominated by a weighted mean** [Wan25, Theorem 1; Cle26; RW,
Theorem 8.4]: for some nonnegative weights `w₀` and `w i` summing to `1`,
`F e ≤ w₀ + ∑ i, w i * e i` at every finite argument `e`. No monotonicity of `F` is assumed.
It is the instance of the complete class theorem for the constraints `e ↦ e i - 1` on the finite
values [Cle26b, closing remark; LRR26, Theorem 3.1]; the derivation is adapted from [EVR26]. -/
theorem IsEMerging.exists_le_add_sum_mul (hF : IsEMerging F) :
    ∃ (w₀ : ℝ≥0) (w : ι → ℝ≥0), w₀ + ∑ i, w i = 1 ∧
      ∀ e : ι → ℝ≥0, F (fun i ↦ e i) ≤ w₀ + ∑ i, (w i : ℝ≥0∞) * e i := by
  -- The complete class theorem on `ι → ℝ≥0`, for the function `e ↦ F e - 1` and the constraints
  -- `e ↦ e i - 1`, which are all negative at `0` and so admit no arbitrage.
  obtain ⟨π, hπ, hle⟩ := exists_mem_nonredundantWeights_le_of_noArbitrageOn
    (noArbitrageOn_of_forall_neg (a := fun (i : ι) (e : ι → ℝ≥0) ↦ (e i : ℝ) - 1)
      (Set.mem_univ 0) fun i ↦ by simp)
    (f := fun e : ι → ℝ≥0 ↦ (F fun i ↦ (e i : ℝ≥0∞)).toReal - 1)
    fun _ _ x _ _ hw hwx ↦ hF.sum_mul_toReal_sub_one_nonpos x hw hwx
  -- The weights of the coordinates are the multipliers, and the constant weight takes the rest.
  set π' : ι → ℝ≥0 := fun i ↦ (π i).toNNReal
  have hπ' (i : ι) : (π' i : ℝ) = π i :=
    Real.coe_toNNReal _ ((mem_nonredundantWeights_iff.1 hπ).1 i)
  have hbound (e : ι → ℝ≥0) :
      (F fun i ↦ e i).toReal ≤ 1 - ∑ i, (π' i : ℝ) + ∑ i, (π' i : ℝ) * e i := by
    have := hle e (Set.mem_univ _)
    simp only [mul_sub, mul_one, Finset.sum_sub_distrib] at this
    simp only [hπ']
    linarith
  have hsum : ∑ i, π' i ≤ 1 := by
    have := (hbound 0).trans' ENNReal.toReal_nonneg
    simp only [Pi.zero_apply, NNReal.coe_zero, mul_zero, Finset.sum_const_zero, add_zero,
      sub_nonneg] at this
    exact_mod_cast this
  refine ⟨1 - ∑ i, π' i, π', tsub_add_cancel_of_le hsum, fun e ↦ ?_⟩
  rw [← coe_add_sum_mul, ← ENNReal.ofReal_toReal (hF.lt_top e).ne,
    ← ENNReal.ofReal_coe_nnreal]
  refine ENNReal.ofReal_le_ofReal ((hbound e).trans_eq ?_)
  rw [NNReal.coe_add, NNReal.coe_sub hsum, NNReal.coe_one, NNReal.coe_sum, NNReal.coe_sum]
  simp only [NNReal.coe_mul]

/-- **E-merging functions are the functions dominated by weighted means**: a measurable function
is e-merging exactly when it is dominated, at finite arguments, by `e ↦ w₀ + ∑ i, w i * e i` for
some nonnegative weights summing to `1`. -/
theorem isEMerging_iff_exists_le_add_sum_mul :
    IsEMerging F ↔ Measurable F ∧ ∃ (w₀ : ℝ≥0) (w : ι → ℝ≥0), w₀ + ∑ i, w i = 1 ∧
      ∀ e : ι → ℝ≥0, F (fun i ↦ e i) ≤ w₀ + ∑ i, (w i : ℝ≥0∞) * e i := by
  refine ⟨fun hF ↦ ⟨hF.measurable, hF.exists_le_add_sum_mul⟩, ?_⟩
  rintro ⟨hF, w₀, w, hw, hle⟩
  exact (isEMerging_add_sum_mul hw.le).of_le hF hle

omit [Fintype ι] in
/-- If a weighted mean is at most another one at every finite argument, then each weight of the
first is at most the corresponding weight of the second. -/
private lemma le_and_forall_le_of_add_sum_mul_le [Fintype ι] {w₀ v₀ : ℝ≥0} {w v : ι → ℝ≥0}
    (h : ∀ e : ι → ℝ≥0, w₀ + ∑ i, w i * e i ≤ v₀ + ∑ i, v i * e i) :
    w₀ ≤ v₀ ∧ ∀ i, w i ≤ v i := by
  classical
  refine ⟨by simpa using h 0, fun i ↦ le_of_not_gt fun hlt ↦ ?_⟩
  -- At the argument `t` on coordinate `i` and `0` elsewhere, for `t` large, the first mean
  -- exceeds the second.
  have hd : (0 : ℝ) < w i - v i := sub_pos.2 (by exact_mod_cast hlt)
  set t : ℝ := (v₀ + 1) / (w i - v i)
  have ht := h fun j ↦ if j = i then t.toNNReal else 0
  simp only [mul_ite, mul_zero, Finset.sum_ite_eq', Finset.mem_univ, ↓reduceIte] at ht
  have ht' : (w₀ : ℝ) + w i * t ≤ v₀ + v i * t := by
    simpa [Real.coe_toNNReal _ (by positivity : 0 ≤ t)] using NNReal.coe_le_coe.2 ht
  have h2 : (w i : ℝ) * t - v i * t = v₀ + 1 := by
    rw [← sub_mul, mul_div_cancel₀ _ hd.ne']
  linarith [NNReal.coe_nonneg w₀]

/-- An **admissible** e-merging function [VW21, §3]: an admissible e-variable for the merging
null of all measures. Its e-variables are the e-merging functions
(`isEMerging_iff_isEVar_mergingNull`) and its quasi-sure filter is the principal filter of the
finite vectors (`quasiSure_mergingNull_univ_eq_principal`), so `F` is admissible when every
e-merging function dominating it at finite arguments equals it there
(`isAdmissibleEMerging_iff_isAdmissible`, `IsAdmissibleEMerging.eq_of_le`). Values at infinite
arguments are not compared, since they do not affect the e-merging property
(`ProbabilityTheory.IsEMerging.of_le`). -/
abbrev IsAdmissibleEMerging (F : (ι → ℝ≥0∞) → ℝ≥0∞) : Prop :=
  IsAdmissibleEVar F (mergingNull Set.univ)

omit [Fintype ι] in
/-- An admissible e-merging function is an e-merging function. -/
theorem IsAdmissibleEMerging.isEMerging (hF : IsAdmissibleEMerging F) : IsEMerging F :=
  isEMerging_iff_isEVar_mergingNull.2 hF.mem

omit [Fintype ι] in
/-- An admissible e-merging function is admissible (`Filter.IsAdmissible`) among the e-merging
functions for the order at finite arguments, the eventual order along the principal filter of the
finite vectors, and conversely. -/
theorem isAdmissibleEMerging_iff_isAdmissible [Finite ι] :
    IsAdmissibleEMerging F ↔
      (Filter.principal (Set.range fun (e : ι → ℝ≥0) i ↦ (e i : ℝ≥0∞))).IsAdmissible
        {G | IsEMerging G} F := by
  simp only [IsAdmissibleEMerging, IsAdmissibleEVar,
    quasiSure_mergingNull_univ_eq_principal, ← isEMerging_iff_isEVar_mergingNull]

omit [Fintype ι] in
/-- An e-merging function that dominates an admissible e-merging function at finite arguments is
equal to it there. -/
theorem IsAdmissibleEMerging.eq_of_le [Finite ι] {G : (ι → ℝ≥0∞) → ℝ≥0∞}
    (hF : IsAdmissibleEMerging F) (hG : IsEMerging G)
    (hFG : ∀ e : ι → ℝ≥0, F (fun i ↦ e i) ≤ G (fun i ↦ e i)) (e : ι → ℝ≥0) :
    G (fun i ↦ e i) = F (fun i ↦ e i) :=
  (Filter.eventuallyLE_principal_range_iff.1
    ((isAdmissibleEMerging_iff_isAdmissible.1 hF).eventuallyLE hG
      (Filter.eventuallyLE_principal_range_iff.2 hFG)) e).antisymm (hFG e)

/-- **The admissible e-merging functions are the weighted means** [Wan25, Theorem 1; RW,
Theorem 8.4]: a function is an admissible e-merging function exactly when it is measurable and
equal, at finite arguments, to `e ↦ w₀ + ∑ i, w i * e i` for some nonnegative weights summing
to `1`. -/
theorem isAdmissibleEMerging_iff :
    IsAdmissibleEMerging F ↔ Measurable F ∧ ∃ (w₀ : ℝ≥0) (w : ι → ℝ≥0), w₀ + ∑ i, w i = 1 ∧
      ∀ e : ι → ℝ≥0, F (fun i ↦ e i) = w₀ + ∑ i, (w i : ℝ≥0∞) * e i := by
  classical
  constructor
  · intro hF
    obtain ⟨w₀, w, hw, hle⟩ := hF.isEMerging.exists_le_add_sum_mul
    exact ⟨hF.isEMerging.measurable, w₀, w, hw,
      fun e ↦ (hF.eq_of_le (isEMerging_add_sum_mul hw.le) hle e).symm⟩
  · rintro ⟨hF, w₀, w, hw, heq⟩
    have hFm : IsEMerging F := (isEMerging_add_sum_mul hw.le).of_le hF fun e ↦ (heq e).le
    refine isAdmissibleEMerging_iff_isAdmissible.2
      ⟨hFm, fun G hG hle ↦ Filter.eventuallyLE_principal_range_iff.2 fun e ↦ ?_⟩
    replace hle := Filter.eventuallyLE_principal_range_iff.1 hle
    -- `G` is dominated by a weighted mean `v`, which dominates the weighted mean `w`; the two
    -- weight vectors are then equal.
    obtain ⟨v₀, v, hv, hGv⟩ := hG.exists_le_add_sum_mul
    have hwv (e : ι → ℝ≥0) : w₀ + ∑ i, w i * e i ≤ v₀ + ∑ i, v i * e i := by
      have := ((heq e).symm.trans_le (hle e)).trans (hGv e)
      rwa [← coe_add_sum_mul, ← coe_add_sum_mul, ENNReal.coe_le_coe] at this
    obtain ⟨hw₀, hwi⟩ := le_and_forall_le_of_add_sum_mul_le hwv
    have hsum : ∑ i, w i ≤ ∑ i, v i := Finset.sum_le_sum fun i _ ↦ hwi i
    obtain ⟨rfl, hsum_eq⟩ := (add_eq_add_iff_eq_and_eq hw₀ hsum).1 (hw.trans hv.symm)
    have hvw : v = w := funext fun i ↦ ((Finset.sum_eq_sum_iff_of_le fun i _ ↦ hwi i).1
      hsum_eq i (Finset.mem_univ i)).symm
    subst hvw
    exact (hGv e).trans_eq (heq e).symm

omit [Fintype ι] in
/-- **Every e-merging function is dominated by an admissible one** [VW21, Proposition E.5], at
finite arguments: by a weighted mean. -/
theorem IsEMerging.exists_isAdmissibleEMerging_le [Finite ι] (hF : IsEMerging F) :
    ∃ G, IsAdmissibleEMerging G ∧ ∀ e : ι → ℝ≥0, F (fun i ↦ e i) ≤ G (fun i ↦ e i) := by
  have := Fintype.ofFinite ι
  obtain ⟨w₀, w, hw, hle⟩ := hF.exists_le_add_sum_mul
  exact ⟨fun e ↦ (w₀ : ℝ≥0∞) + ∑ i, (w i : ℝ≥0∞) * e i, isAdmissibleEMerging_iff.2
    ⟨by fun_prop, w₀, w, hw, fun e ↦ rfl⟩, hle⟩

open Filter in
/-- **The weighted means form a minimal complete class of e-merging functions**: every e-merging
function is dominated at finite arguments by a mean `e ↦ w₀ + ∑ i, w i * e i` with nonnegative
weights summing to `1`, and none of these means can be removed together with the functions equal
to it at finite arguments. The means are the admissible e-merging functions [Wan25, Theorem 1],
and a complete class of admissible elements is minimal (`Filter.isMinimalCompleteClass_iff`). -/
theorem isMinimalCompleteClass_add_sum_mul :
    (𝓟 (Set.range fun (e : ι → ℝ≥0) i ↦ (e i : ℝ≥0∞))).IsMinimalCompleteClass {G | IsEMerging G}
      {G | ∃ (w₀ : ℝ≥0) (w : ι → ℝ≥0), w₀ + ∑ i, w i = 1 ∧
        G = fun e ↦ w₀ + ∑ i, (w i : ℝ≥0∞) * e i} := by
  refine IsCompleteClass.isMinimalCompleteClass ⟨?_, fun F hF ↦ ?_⟩ ?_
  · rintro _ ⟨w₀, w, hw, rfl⟩
    exact isEMerging_add_sum_mul hw.le
  · obtain ⟨w₀, w, hw, hle⟩ := IsEMerging.exists_le_add_sum_mul hF
    exact ⟨_, ⟨w₀, w, hw, rfl⟩, eventuallyLE_principal_range_iff.2 hle⟩
  · rintro _ ⟨w₀, w, hw, rfl⟩
    exact isAdmissibleEMerging_iff_isAdmissible.1
      (isAdmissibleEMerging_iff.2 ⟨by fun_prop, w₀, w, hw, fun e ↦ rfl⟩)

/-! ### Symmetric e-merging functions -/

/-- `λ + (1 - λ) M`, for the arithmetic mean `M`, is the weighted mean with the weight
`(1 - λ) / K` on each of the `K` coordinates. -/
private lemma mul_sum_div_card_eq_sum_mul (lam : ℝ≥0) (e : ι → ℝ≥0) :
    (1 - lam) * ((∑ i, e i) / Fintype.card ι) = ∑ i, (1 - lam) / Fintype.card ι * e i := by
  rw [← Finset.mul_sum, div_mul_eq_mul_div, mul_div_assoc]

/-- The arithmetic mean of finite arguments has the same value computed in `ℝ≥0` and in
`ℝ≥0∞`. -/
private lemma coe_sum_div_card (e : ι → ℝ≥0) :
    (((∑ i, e i) / Fintype.card ι : ℝ≥0) : ℝ≥0∞) = (∑ i, (e i : ℝ≥0∞)) / Fintype.card ι := by
  rcases isEmpty_or_nonempty ι with hι | hι
  · simp
  · rw [ENNReal.coe_div (by simp [Fintype.card_ne_zero]), ENNReal.ofNNReal_finsetSum,
      ENNReal.coe_natCast]

/-- `λ + (1 - λ) M`, for the arithmetic mean `M` of finite arguments, has the same value computed
in `ℝ≥0` and in `ℝ≥0∞`. -/
private lemma coe_add_mul_sum_div_card (lam : ℝ≥0) (e : ι → ℝ≥0) :
    ((lam + (1 - lam) * ((∑ i, e i) / Fintype.card ι) : ℝ≥0) : ℝ≥0∞) =
      lam + (1 - lam) * ((∑ i, (e i : ℝ≥0∞)) / Fintype.card ι) := by
  rw [ENNReal.coe_add, ENNReal.coe_mul, ENNReal.coe_sub, ENNReal.coe_one, coe_sum_div_card]

/-- **Symmetrizing a dominating weighted mean**: if `F` is symmetric at finite arguments and
dominated there by the weighted mean with weights `w₀` and `w i`, then it is also dominated by the
weighted mean with weights `w₀` and the averages `(∑ σ, w (σ j)) / N` over the `N` permutations
`σ`. -/
private lemma le_add_sum_div_card_mul_of_symmetric [DecidableEq ι] {w₀ : ℝ≥0} {w : ι → ℝ≥0}
    (hsymm : ∀ (σ : Equiv.Perm ι) (e : ι → ℝ≥0), F (fun i ↦ e (σ i)) = F (fun i ↦ e i))
    (hle : ∀ e : ι → ℝ≥0, F (fun i ↦ e i) ≤ w₀ + ∑ i, (w i : ℝ≥0∞) * e i) (e : ι → ℝ≥0) :
    F (fun i ↦ e i) ≤
      ↑(w₀ + ∑ j, (∑ σ : Equiv.Perm ι, w (σ j)) / Fintype.card (Equiv.Perm ι) * e j) := by
  set N : ℝ≥0 := (Fintype.card (Equiv.Perm ι) : ℝ≥0)
  have hN : N ≠ 0 := by simp [N, Fintype.card_ne_zero]
  -- `F e = F (e ∘ σ⁻¹)` is dominated by the weighted mean with the weights `w ∘ σ`.
  have hσ (σ : Equiv.Perm ι) : F (fun i ↦ e i) ≤ ↑(w₀ + ∑ j, w (σ j) * e j) := by
    refine ((hsymm σ⁻¹ e).symm.trans_le (hle _)).trans_eq ?_
    rw [coe_add_sum_mul, ← Equiv.sum_comp σ fun j ↦ (w j : ℝ≥0∞) * e (σ⁻¹ j)]
    simp
  -- Average these bounds over the permutations `σ`.
  have hsum : (N : ℝ≥0∞) * F (fun i ↦ e i) ≤
      ↑(∑ σ : Equiv.Perm ι, (w₀ + ∑ j, w (σ j) * e j)) := by
    have := Finset.card_nsmul_le_sum Finset.univ _ _ fun σ _ ↦ hσ σ
    rwa [Finset.card_univ, nsmul_eq_mul, ← ENNReal.coe_natCast,
      ← ENNReal.ofNNReal_finsetSum] at this
  have hcalc : (∑ σ : Equiv.Perm ι, (w₀ + ∑ j, w (σ j) * e j)) / N =
      w₀ + ∑ j, (∑ σ : Equiv.Perm ι, w (σ j)) / N * e j := by
    rw [Finset.sum_add_distrib, Finset.sum_const, Finset.card_univ, nsmul_eq_mul, add_div,
      mul_div_cancel_left₀ _ hN, Finset.sum_comm, Finset.sum_div]
    simp only [← Finset.sum_mul, mul_div_right_comm]
  rw [← hcalc, ENNReal.coe_div hN, ENNReal.le_div_iff_mul_le (Or.inl (by simpa using hN))
    (Or.inl ENNReal.coe_ne_top), mul_comm]
  exact hsum

/-- **A symmetric e-merging function is dominated by a mixture of `1` and the arithmetic mean**
[VW21, Theorem 3.2]: for some `λ ∈ [0, 1]`, `F e ≤ λ + (1 - λ) * (∑ i, e i) / K` at every finite
argument `e`, with `K` the number of e-values. Symmetry is needed only at finite arguments. -/
theorem IsEMerging.exists_le_add_mul_sum_div_card (hF : IsEMerging F)
    (hsymm : ∀ (σ : Equiv.Perm ι) (e : ι → ℝ≥0), F (fun i ↦ e (σ i)) = F (fun i ↦ e i)) :
    ∃ lam ≤ (1 : ℝ≥0), ∀ e : ι → ℝ≥0,
      F (fun i ↦ e i) ≤ lam + (1 - lam) * ((∑ i, (e i : ℝ≥0∞)) / Fintype.card ι) := by
  classical
  obtain ⟨w₀, w, hw, hle⟩ := hF.exists_le_add_sum_mul
  refine ⟨w₀, hw ▸ le_self_add, fun e ↦ ?_⟩
  rw [← coe_add_mul_sum_div_card, mul_sum_div_card_eq_sum_mul]
  refine (le_add_sum_div_card_mul_of_symmetric hsymm hle e).trans_eq ?_
  -- The averaged weights are all equal to `(1 - w₀) / K`.
  simp_rw [Equiv.Perm.sum_apply_div_card]
  rw [← hw, add_tsub_cancel_left]

/-- **The admissible symmetric e-merging functions** [VW21, Theorem 3.2]: a function of at least
one e-value, symmetric at finite arguments, is an admissible e-merging function exactly when it is
measurable and equal, at finite arguments, to `λ + (1 - λ) * (∑ i, e i) / K` for some
`λ ∈ [0, 1]`. -/
theorem isAdmissibleEMerging_iff_of_symmetric [Nonempty ι]
    (hsymm : ∀ (σ : Equiv.Perm ι) (e : ι → ℝ≥0), F (fun i ↦ e (σ i)) = F (fun i ↦ e i)) :
    IsAdmissibleEMerging F ↔ Measurable F ∧ ∃ lam ≤ (1 : ℝ≥0), ∀ e : ι → ℝ≥0,
      F (fun i ↦ e i) = lam + (1 - lam) * ((∑ i, (e i : ℝ≥0∞)) / Fintype.card ι) := by
  have hcard : (Fintype.card ι : ℝ≥0) ≠ 0 := by simp [Fintype.card_ne_zero]
  have hweights (lam : ℝ≥0) (hlam : lam ≤ 1) :
      lam + ∑ _i : ι, (1 - lam) / Fintype.card ι = 1 := by
    rw [Finset.sum_const, Finset.card_univ, nsmul_eq_mul, mul_div_cancel₀ _ hcard,
      add_tsub_cancel_of_le hlam]
  -- `λ + (1 - λ) M` is the weighted mean with the weight `(1 - λ) / K` on each coordinate.
  have hGe (lam : ℝ≥0) (e : ι → ℝ≥0) :
      (lam : ℝ≥0∞) + ∑ i, (((1 - lam) / Fintype.card ι : ℝ≥0) : ℝ≥0∞) * e i =
        lam + (1 - lam) * ((∑ i, (e i : ℝ≥0∞)) / Fintype.card ι) := by
    rw [← coe_add_mul_sum_div_card, mul_sum_div_card_eq_sum_mul, coe_add_sum_mul]
  constructor
  · intro hF
    obtain ⟨lam, hlam, hle⟩ := hF.isEMerging.exists_le_add_mul_sum_div_card hsymm
    refine ⟨hF.isEMerging.measurable, lam, hlam, fun e ↦ ?_⟩
    have hG := isEMerging_add_sum_mul (hweights lam hlam).le
    rw [← hGe]
    exact (hF.eq_of_le hG (fun e ↦ (hle e).trans_eq (hGe lam e).symm) e).symm
  · rintro ⟨hF, lam, hlam, heq⟩
    exact isAdmissibleEMerging_iff.2 ⟨hF, lam, fun _ ↦ (1 - lam) / Fintype.card ι,
      hweights lam hlam, fun e ↦ (heq e).trans (hGe lam e).symm⟩

/-- **The arithmetic mean essentially dominates every symmetric e-merging function** [VW21,
Proposition 3.1; RW, Proposition 8.3]: wherever an e-merging function symmetric at finite
arguments exceeds `1`, it is at most the arithmetic mean `(∑ i, e i) / K`. -/
theorem IsEMerging.le_sum_div_card_of_symmetric (hF : IsEMerging F)
    (hsymm : ∀ (σ : Equiv.Perm ι) (e : ι → ℝ≥0), F (fun i ↦ e (σ i)) = F (fun i ↦ e i))
    {e : ι → ℝ≥0} (he : 1 < F (fun i ↦ e i)) :
    F (fun i ↦ e i) ≤ (∑ i, (e i : ℝ≥0∞)) / Fintype.card ι := by
  obtain ⟨lam, hlam, hle⟩ := hF.exists_le_add_mul_sum_div_card hsymm
  have hle' := (hle e).trans_eq (coe_add_mul_sum_div_card lam e).symm
  rw [← coe_sum_div_card]
  set m := (∑ i, e i) / (Fintype.card ι : ℝ≥0)
  refine hle'.trans (ENNReal.coe_le_coe.2 ?_)
  -- If `m ≤ 1`, the bound `λ + (1 - λ) m` is at most `1`, which `F e` exceeds.
  have hm : 1 ≤ m := by
    refine le_of_not_gt fun hm ↦ ?_
    have : lam + (1 - lam) * m ≤ 1 := by
      calc lam + (1 - lam) * m ≤ lam + (1 - lam) * 1 := by gcongr
        _ = 1 := by rw [mul_one, add_tsub_cancel_of_le hlam]
    exact (he.trans_le hle').not_ge (by exact_mod_cast this)
  calc lam + (1 - lam) * m ≤ lam * m + (1 - lam) * m := by
        gcongr
        exact le_mul_of_one_le_right' hm
    _ = m := by rw [← add_mul, add_tsub_cancel_of_le hlam, one_mul]

end ProbabilityTheory
